00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:14.699
Mark Zuckerberg, CEO of Facebook, and dear
friend to 100 million real, human, people,

00:00:14.699 --> 00:00:16.230
has an announcement.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.470
First, just a reminder, this is a good company
- we do good things here - like providing

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.240
a public space to share news and information.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.650
But, did you know, sometimes people also like
talking in private?

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.999
Weird, but we’re here to serve you.

00:00:30.999 --> 00:00:35.460
If the world wants more private messaging,
Facebook will provide it!

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.510
Doing this is not easy, by the way.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.539
We’re making huge sacrifices over here!

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:44.989
And, sure, we’re not perfect, we’ve still
got lots of work to do - so don’t blame

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:50.579
us when we inevitably mess this up, and don’t
expect any real, tangible changes anytime

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:51.579
soon.

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:56.420
Now, if you’re like me, you saw this headline,
yawned, chuckled, rolled your eyes, and got

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.800
back to watching a robot drive a robot across
the desert in silence.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.739
But I think that’s a mistake.

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:04.160
The first part, I mean.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.040
Right now, three of the biggest companies
on earth - Facebook, Google, and Apple - are

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.150
telling vastly different, conflicting stories
about your privacy.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:17.610
So, who can you really trust?

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.540
Marketing 101: Every company is telling a
story.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:27.180
About who you are, who you should be, and,
more specifically, why 40 pounds of dehydrated

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:33.280
marshmallows, which, double as a soft pillow-filling
material, will help you become that person.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.490
Some stories are subtle, others… not.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.860
And some can coexist.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:43.750
Everyone loves a good Apple versus Google
headline, but, the truth is, on the whole,

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:47.750
the two have probably been more symbiotic
than competitive.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:52.540
One sells advertisements, and the other, screens
through which to see them, disproportionately

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.900
bought by an advertiser’s favorite demographic:
young people with disposable income.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.420
Although, this is kind of just an accident.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:06.460
A few years ago, Apple made it really easy
to block ads on your iPhone, which, for them,

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.129
is a nice, but not really earth-shattering
feature.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:15.560
For Google and the rest of the advertising
industry, it was ahhh, less than ideal.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:21.129
I guess the lesson is don’t let 75% of your
revenue come from a company with totally opposite

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:22.450
priorities.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:27.459
The human was just crossing the street, but
that’s the end of the world for the ant.

00:02:27.459 --> 00:02:31.990
Today, the only difference is that each company
is much more actively trying to step on the

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:36.140
other, with their mutually-exclusive stories
about collecting your data.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:40.540
First, Google, embraces it - arguing that
you shouldn’t just tolerate their using

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.819
your data, you should want it.

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:46.840
One because giving your phone more information
makes it more useful.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:51.370
Your phone is your secretary, and the more
it knows about you, you more it can do for

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.370
you.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:56.560
It’s easy to say you value your privacy,
people overwhelmingly do.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:01.079
But, then, in practice, do you really care
that Google can read your email if it means

00:03:01.079 --> 00:03:05.519
you can tell your phone to “book a car”
and, because it already knows when and where,

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:07.829
it automatically does it for you?

00:03:07.829 --> 00:03:10.790
People have certainly sold their data for
a lot less.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:15.639
And, two, because all this data, in aggregate,
makes these services better, cheaper, and

00:03:15.639 --> 00:03:19.250
more accessible to, say, people in poverty.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.099
Critics argue these tools aren’t really
free, you just pay with data instead of dollars.

00:03:24.099 --> 00:03:29.099
Well, says Google, if data has value, then
donating it is charity.

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:34.049
Let Google see your photos, and Translate
gets better at reading text, which helps disadvantaged

00:03:34.049 --> 00:03:35.860
people navigate the world.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.989
Really, you're a hero.

00:03:37.989 --> 00:03:43.029
Everything from Gmail, to Chrome, Photos,
Drive, and Translate, relies on collecting

00:03:43.029 --> 00:03:48.340
your data, and, thus, convincing people of
one or both of these stories.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.530
Apple, meanwhile, rejects the whole concept.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.749
Tim Cook argues that’s a fake trade-off
designed to justify a business model where

00:03:55.749 --> 00:03:58.489
you are the product, not the customer.

00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:03.480
Not only does your iPhone not need your data
to be useful, it says, it doesn’t even want

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.480
it.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.480
For Apple, storing your information is only
a liability.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.329
Now, whether you buy that logic or not, you
have to stop and admire its genius.

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:17.100
Because, if Google says your data is what
allows it to sell cheaper products, then Apple

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.380
can argue it’s higher prices are a feature.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.381
You should feel good paying more for an iPhone,
because it’s proof Apple doesn’t need

00:04:24.381 --> 00:04:26.540
to sell you out to advertisers.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.950
On the other hand, this argument is also harder
to explain.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:35.720
While Tim is busy waxing poetic about privacy,
Google just points to the price tag - everyone

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.360
wants to save money.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.080
And, finally, Facebook, denies it.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.030
“The Future is Private”, it says, so while
the old website emphasized the News Feed - an

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:46.890
open, public place to talk,

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:49.920
The “Town Square” is becoming more like
a “living room”.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.390
Now, with the redesign, it’s all about private
groups and communities.

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:58.600
Notice, by “private” it means ‘exclusive’
or ‘separate’, not necessarily “your

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.650
data stays between you and your device, or
you and the receiver”.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:07.500
In other words, it gets to capitalize on the
buzzword - like ‘Cloud’, ‘AI’ or ‘blockchain’

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.080
before it, without having to make any significant
changes.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.370
Google also announced something new:

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:17.840
The idea is that, say, you’re typing a new
acronym - instead of sending that data to

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.271
Google’s servers to determine whether it’s
a new word to add to its dictionary, or just

00:05:22.271 --> 00:05:25.990
a typo, your phone itself computes that locally.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:30.700
This way your phone can make use of your data,
without Google, the company, being able to

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:31.700
see it.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:35.930
It’s similar to Apple’s “Differential
Privacy”, with just as bad a name.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:37.280
It’s interesting because:

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.560
A) Unrelated to all this, phones are getting
so good that people don’t feel the need

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.050
to upgrade so often.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:48.140
Using the A12 chip on the iPhone XS to send
Snapchats is kind of like using a Ford Super

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:50.310
Duty to haul your child’s teddy bear.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:55.470
But, when more work is being done on-device,
rather than, in the cloud, all that power

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:56.850
is suddenly useful.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:02.530
and, B), If Google can have its cake and eat
it too - give you both privacy and a better

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:05.240
product, then Apple just looks more expensive.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.650
That’s why these companies are so enthusiastic
all the sudden about privacy.

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:13.310
No-one forced their hand so much as gave them
an opportunity.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:17.750
The priorities of a growing company, like
Facebook in 2010 when it got caught sharing

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:22.220
your private information, are very different
from those of an established, dominant one,

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.650
like Facebook today.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:28.370
A growing company is much more willing to
move fast and break things because that’s

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:29.370
what it takes.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:33.650
It’s easy to do a bad thing, get big by
doing it, and then say “Look at us, aren’t

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.280
we cool for not doing that bad thing anymore!”

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:37.660
See: Uber.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:43.240
It may be strategically smarter to fix a problem
later than do the right thing all along.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.880
Facebook got where it is today by eating up
your data.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:50.350
But, now, it’s an open question whether
it really needs it anymore.

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:54.530
Even if every Facebook message were encrypted
tomorrow, it would still be able to show you

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:55.930
relevant ads.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:56.930
How?

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:02.600
Because they had years to train those algorithms
with the data they now so courageously encrypt.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:06.860
So they get PR-points for doing what they
would’ve done anyway: try to draw back the

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:11.840
kids these days who’ve left in favor of
Instagram, Discord, and Snapchat.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.860
Facebook and Google have the same basic business
model: selling tailored advertising.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:21.330
The difference is, the latter provides way
more value, so it can afford to be much more

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:22.730
honest about the trade.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:25.770
So, am I saying don’t trust Facebook?

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:26.770
Yes.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:30.700
Not because Mark Zuckerberg is evil, more
like “detached from reality”.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:35.590
Now, don’t get me wrong, sometimes I like
my CEO’s detached from reality.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:40.540
It’s just that someone sober needs to trip
sit so things don’t get out of control.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:45.010
Usually we call that a board of directors,
but when the CEO has the power to appoint

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:48.880
and fire board members, well, that kinda defeats
the purpose.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:52.160
The problem is Facebook has three kinds of
shares,

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.111
Class A - which are so generously offered
for purchase to you and me, come with 1 vote

00:07:57.111 --> 00:07:58.111
each.

00:07:58.111 --> 00:07:59.990
Class B, 10 votes.

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:02.730
And Class C, come with no voting rights.

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:04.930
Can you guess which kind Mark gives to charity?

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:12.530
So, even though, on paper, Zuckerberg “only”
owns about 28% of shares, he has about 53.3%

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:16.460
of all the voting power, granting him majority
control.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:21.580
Because of this, Facebook’s board of directors
has about as much power as North Korea’s

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:22.580
parliament.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:26.570
Zuckerberg will be voted out right after Kim
Jong-Un loses an election.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.840
Okay, so what about Apple?

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.890
Does it really care about your privacy?

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:32.930
Personally, I hope not.

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:36.789
Much better the reason they build secure,
private products is that they’re financially

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:38.389
incentivized to.

00:08:38.389 --> 00:08:45.080
Good, caring, trustworthy people retire, get
fired, and pass away, but incentives, I can

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:46.080
trust.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.540
People who proclaim ‘Apple just wants your
money’ are missing the point…

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:53.269
Who knows what Apple’s true motivations
are?, whatever that means.

00:08:53.269 --> 00:08:55.269
But, also, who cares!

00:08:55.269 --> 00:08:59.459
Zuckerberg once said “I think it’s important
that we don’t all get Stockholm Syndrome,

00:08:59.459 --> 00:09:02.820
and let the companies that work hard to charge
you more, convince you that they actually

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:04.319
care more about you.”

00:09:04.319 --> 00:09:08.680
And, I agree - billion-dollar companies tend
to be kinda... single-minded.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:11.290
So, follow the business model.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:14.770
The only thing you should trust is that a
company will try to do what’s in its best

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:16.060
financial interest.

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:20.699
So, make sure their’s is, at least, kinda
aligned with yours.

00:09:20.699 --> 00:09:25.600
Whenever there’s a big hack, Tim Cook will,
predictably, do a few interviews about privacy,

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.579
trying to convince you that Apple’s interests
are most aligned with yours.

00:09:29.579 --> 00:09:33.760
With every update, Google, will try to do
the same, by giving you more features and

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.990
more value in exchange for your data.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:38.800
And, Facebook, will… keep apologizing.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.990
Now, it’s just a question of whose story
you buy.

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:47.529
The surprising thing about Facebook is that
it doesn’t really need that much information

00:09:47.529 --> 00:09:48.949
to know who you are.

00:09:48.949 --> 00:09:53.890
As the probability lesson on Brilliant.org
explains, a little bit of data has a lot of

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:55.069
predictive power.

00:09:55.069 --> 00:09:59.899
For example, Facebook sees you’re visiting
its website from Berkeley, California, a city

00:09:59.899 --> 00:10:01.749
of 122,000 people.

00:10:01.749 --> 00:10:07.550
But, you also joined an expecting mother’s
Facebook group, and recently traveled to Vancouver.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:12.569
There are lots of pregnant Californians and
many people recently visited Vancouver, but,

00:10:12.569 --> 00:10:17.800
together, the overlap is just small enough
to guess who, exactly, you are.

